A city in the Pacific Northwest has been adding a lot more housing supply (especially rental), and it seems like it may be working out pretty well. Here's a news article from this week:

[Local renters score big as landlords dangle freebies to fill empty apartments - Local News Outlet](https://www.example.com/business/real-estate/free-amazon-echo-2-months-free-rent-2500-gift-cards-seattle-apartment-glut-gives-renters-freebies/)

&gt;**As new apartments open across the city in record numbers, vacancy rates have grown, rents have stopped rising and landlords are offering an increasing number of freebies to get tenants in the door.**  
&gt;  
&gt;For years, the local rental market was so heated that renters would constantly monitor online listings and quickly show up to available apartments with checkbooks and references in hand. For many apartments, they’d have to fill out applications on the spot and cross their fingers.  
&gt;  
&gt;Those days are over. A glut of new apartments washing over the city has quickly turned the tables as vacancy rates hit their highest levels since the recession, led by the downtown area, where one-fourth of all apartments are now sitting empty.  
&gt;  
&gt;**Landlords who are increasingly hard-pressed to fill their open apartments are offering deals like a free month’s rent, lucrative gift cards and even free electronics.**  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;**This is all happening because of the region’s record apartment-construction boom. Overall, the city is getting more new units this half-decade than in the previous 50 years combined, and the peak of the openings is happening right now.**  
&gt;  
&gt;**Even in 2016, the metropolitan area was already building the most new apartments per capita of any big metro area in the country, according to a study by a housing research firm — and construction has only sped up since then.**  
&gt;  
&gt;The apartment openings have finally begun to outpace the region’s nation-leading population growth. Rental vacancy rates have grown, particularly in areas where lots of new apartments are going — namely, in and around downtown.  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;**This is all translating to somewhat better news for tenants on the rents they pay. The city was among the top six metro areas in the country for rent growth every year from 2015 to 2017, according to the housing research firm. But this year? It’s 22nd of out 25, and rents here have actually dropped slightly compared with the record highs reached last summer.**

Funny enough, in that local news outlet comment section, it's all NIMBYs saying "I guess the problem's solved, so don't you dare upzone my neighbourhood too!"

Anyway, I think rezoning to allow more housing is probably the most useful thing that can be done at the municipal level, to help housing affordability. Particularly additional rental housing IMO.

I support demand-side policies as well, but most of those policy levers are at the provincial level (and fortunately, the new provincial government has been taking steps in that regard).

&gt;The revenue lost by the tax cuts are made up by more people being employed and paying taxes instead of relying on social assistance.

They always make that claim, but it never turns out that way.  The deficit is spiking due to the current administration's tax cut, just as it did when a previous Republican president cut taxes, and just as it did when another Republican president cut taxes.  (Though for what it's worth, I think the earlier president's tax cuts were the most justifiable of the three)

Here's what the US Congressional Budget Office found in April:

[ Federal Budget Deficit Projected to Soar to Over $1 Trillion in 2020 - NYT](https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/09/us/politics/federal-deficit-tax-cuts-spending-trump.html)

&gt;WASHINGTON — **The federal government’s annual budget deficit is set to widen significantly in the next few years, and is expected to top $1 trillion in 2020 despite healthy economic growth**, according to [new projections](https://www.cbo.gov/publication/53651) from the nonpartisan Congressional Budget Office released Monday.  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;The budget office forecast is the first since the current president signed a sweeping tax overhaul, then signed legislation to significantly increase military and domestic spending over the next two years. The figures are sobering, even in a political climate where deficit concerns appear to be receding.  
&gt;  
&gt;The tax overhaul, which includes permanent tax cuts for corporations and temporary ones for individuals, will increase the size of the economy by an average of 0.7 percent from 2018 to 2028, according to the budget office.  
&gt;  
&gt;**But that added economic growth does not come close to paying for the tax overhaul, which the budget office said would add more than $1.8 trillion to deficits over that period, from lost tax revenue and higher interest payments.**  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;**This fiscal year \[2018\], which ends Sept. 30, the budget deficit is expected to total $804 billion, up from $665 billion last fiscal year \[2017\]**, according to the projections. In a decade, the red ink is expected to reach $1.5 trillion.

To put things into perspective, the $800 billion deficit in America is equivalent to a $80 billion deficit in a country with Canada's population.  By comparison, the Canadian prime minister gets criticism for running a $17 billion deficit, and the largest deficit in Canadian history was $50 billion (during the recession in 2009/10).  Just think about that.

To me, it's completely irresponsible for the current president and the Republican congress to be cutting taxes and reducing revenue, at a time when unemployment is already low (minimal slack in the economy), and there is already a huge budget deficit.

If they cared about debt, they should be raising taxes and lowering spending at this time.  The economy is currently strong, so it's an ideal time to take those measures and reduce the deficit.  And then whenever the next recession hits?  *That* is when it would be a good time to cut taxes and/or increase spending, as a way to provide stimulus and temporarily boost the economy.

I thought this article was an interesting and amusing read.

&amp;#x200B;

&gt;Traffic anarchy. Head-on collisions. Confused horses.   
&gt;  
&gt;No one knew what to expect on Dec. 1, 1922, when a Canadian province switched to the right-hand rule of the road at the stroke of midnight.   
&gt;  
&gt;For as long as anyone could remember, drivers in the region had obeyed the left-hand rule of the road — handed down from the region's colonial past.   
&gt;  
