&amp;#x200B;

For those who don't want to read the whole article, here are some key passages from it, explaining what it's all about:

&amp;#x200B;

&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;Generally, federal employees have been free to express opinions about policies and legislative activity at work as long as they do not advocate voting for or against particular candidates in partisan elections.  
&gt;  
&gt;But in a guidance document distributed on Wednesday, the independent agency that enforces the Hatch Act, a law that bars federal employees from taking part in partisan political campaigns at work or in an official capacity, warned that making or displaying statements at work about impeaching or resisting the current president is likely to amount to illegal political activity.  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;The reasoning behind the guidance centers on the fact that the current president is already running for re-election. It contends that arguments about his policies or impeachment prospects are effectively statements in support or opposition to his campaign.  
&gt;  
&gt;“We understand that the ‘resistance’ and ‘#resist’ originally gained prominence shortly after the president’s election and generally related to efforts to oppose administration policies,” the guidance said. “However, ‘resistance,’ ‘#resist’ and similar terms have become inextricably linked with the electoral success (or failure) of the president.”  
&gt;  
&gt;And while impeachment is primarily about removing a president from office, the agency said that because a removed president would also apparently become disqualified from holding federal office in the future, supporting or opposing the current president’s impeachment amounts to taking a stand on his potential re-election.  
&gt;  
&gt;“Advocating for a candidate to be impeached, and thus potentially disqualified from holding federal office, is clearly directed at the failure of that candidate’s campaign for federal office,” the guidance said. “Similarly, advocating against a candidate’s impeachment is activity directed at maintaining that candidate’s eligibility for federal office and therefore also considered political activity.”  
&gt;  
&gt;Several legal specialists raised concerns about the new guidance, warning that it would intimidate people into avoiding even casual discussions with colleagues that should not be deemed banned by the statute.  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;The chief of the Office of Special Counsel’s Hatch Act unit, who has held the position for many years, said she and her deputy came up with the interpretation after receiving numerous questions about the rules. She argued that the guidance fit within the office’s past interpretations, while noting that the current president’s presidency has raised new wrinkles: The two presidents subjected to impeachment proceedings since the Hatch Act became law, who served in their second terms, were ineligible to run for president again anyway.  
&gt;  
&gt;The chief also said the Hatch Act often raises “gray area” situations, in which investigators have to carefully consider all the facts and circumstances before deciding whether a line has been crossed. She contrasted what she deemed the clear case of someone who displays a sign or button calling for impeachment at work with the murkier case of someone who says that while discussing the news with a colleague over lunch in a federal building.  
&gt;  
&gt;Still, a law professor at a university in St. Louis who specializes in government ethics issues, criticized the idea of bootstrapping the notion of impeachment to the upcoming election at all.  
&gt;  
&gt;“This goes beyond past guidance about what partisan political activity is, and is more restrictive of speech of federal employees than past guidance that I’ve been able to find,” she said, adding: “I think their legal analysis is wrong in this attempt to outlaw all discussion of impeachment of the current president in the federal workplace. Maybe that is a good idea, maybe that is a bad idea, but I don’t think that is what the Hatch Act requires."

&amp;#x200B;

Voting is mostly along party lines, but that's the last stage of the process.  You could argue that the more important part is determining what's in the legislation (and what the party line is on that legislation) and legislators get to play a role in that.  They get to directly review and revise legislation through committee work, and they can try to influence the party stances on issues through caucus discussions, meetings with ministers, etc.

&nbsp;

And they do have a role on resolving local matters, but I'm not entirely sure how that works, so perhaps someone else can explain that better than me.

