&gt;In 1988, the federal minister responsible for the intelligence agency at the time directed the spy service to sort through the resulting heap of files.  
&gt;  
&gt;Some police records – including voluminous files on a provincial premier and national political leaders – were sent to the national archives.  
&gt;  
&gt;Others were destroyed, including dossiers on former prime ministers. Still other files, judged to have current value at the time, went to the agency’s active intelligence holdings.  
&gt;  
&gt;Security records on individuals become eligible for disclosure under access laws only 20 years after a person’s death. Until then, even the existence of a file is secret due to privacy considerations.  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;A news agency recently requested a former prime minister’s police file under access laws from the national archives and the intelligence agency prior to the 20th anniversary of his passing, given that it can take many months to process such applications.  
&gt;  
&gt;**The archives swiftly replied that it does not have a dossier on the former leader. The intelligence agency said its records indicate the file was destroyed shortly after the sorting directive.**  
&gt;  
&gt;**In a written response to questions, the spy service said an analysis of the file concluded it did not meet the threshold to justify being kept in the agency’s active inventory. The file also fell short of criteria for preservation set out by the national archives and was therefore destroyed the following year, the agency added.**  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;The intelligence agency declined to elaborate on the rationale for purging the file.  
&gt;  
&gt;However, when destruction of files on other former leaders came to light years ago, the spy service noted they were presumably compiled at the height of the Cold War.  
