&amp;#x200B;

&gt;In February 2018, a provincial finance minister announced that  it was the stated policy of her government to force down home prices.  Together with a suite of initiatives announced at that time, she made it  clear that the policy of the ruling party wasn’t just to moderate home-price  increases, but to enact whatever policy initiatives were necessary to  bring them down.  
&gt;  
&gt;With this announcement, the province entered unchartered  territory. For the first time in history, a government in Canada  announced that its policy was to reduce the wealth of its constituents.  About 68 per cent of residents [own their own homes](https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/daily-quotidien/171025/cg-c002-eng.htm) and for the vast majority it’s the primary repository of their wealth and savings.  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;First, it can’t be denied that it has been wildly successful. Never in  history has the region lost so much wealth so quickly. Recent reports are that  [$90 billion has been lost in home value](https://globalnews.ca/news/5300334/analysis-metro-vancouver-home-equity-market-slides/),  just in the metropolitan area, just over the past year. This figure is  roughly 65 per cent of the entire annual economic output of the region, which has a GDP of $137 billion. It represents about $40,000 for every  resident of the metro area in the year, almost matching average annual salaries.  
&gt;  
&gt;...  
&gt;  
&gt;Second, the real-estate development and construction industries — more  than 20 per cent of the provincial economy — are facing a significant downturn. A political advisory group’s April 2019 [Pre-Sale Real Estate Insights](https://mlacanada.com/newsfeed/april-2019-pre-sale-real-estate-insights)  reports that about 5,000 concrete units within 17 development projects  have been postponed. Housing starts are down by at least 20 per cent.  Further project cancellations are announced weekly. Billions in  investment has already been lost, with job losses and an associated  decline in economic activity to follow.  
&gt;  
&gt;**Third, one is hard-pressed to find the winners of this policy. Despite  the massive wealth destruction and economic damage, few younger residents are now celebrating that they can finally afford a home. Nor would it make  sense for any of them to buy one so long as it’s the stated policy of  their government to destroy the savings they invest.**

&amp;#x200B;

The bolded paragraph there is particularly amusing... he's arguing that younger residents actually aren't happy about falling real estate prices, because they're so worried that if they buy in, the ruling party will continue bringing down real estate prices and destroy their housing wealth.  By that logic, those people could just wait on the sidelines as real estate keeps coming down, and it's really not clear why they wouldn't be happy with that turn of events.

&gt;A major energy company stepped away from the project as it was not economically feasible

A major energy company stepped away because as of this time last year, the provincial government was trying to fight it (their efforts have since been denied in the courts), and there was an legal challenge underway from indigenous groups (which was successful in overturning the pipeline's first approval, which led to 6+ months of additional review, and then eventually this new approval).

&amp;#x200B;

Now that those two hurdles have been cleared, the project would presumably be economically feasible - hence why there seems to be private sector interest in potentially purchasing it.

&amp;#x200B;

Whether or not pipeline expansion is the right direction for society is open for debate, but in terms of economic circumstances, uncertainty, and risk, the pipeline project is in a very different place now, compared to one year ago.

&gt;I'm not pretending the output-based pricing system part of the program doesn't exist.

Then it's confusing why you'd claim that emissions would get off-shored to a major manufacturing nation, if you know about the output-based pricing system, which is designed specifically to prevent that.  What industries do you think are going to relocate?

&amp;#x200B;

&amp;#x200B;

&gt;In fact, the output-based pricing system allows certain industries that are heavy carbon emitters to get a free pass so they can stay competitive.

&amp;#x200B;

If a company in those industries can produce 20% more efficiently than the industry average (in terms of emissions-per-production), then they get a free pass.  If not, they pay based on how much worse they are than that threshold.

&amp;#x200B;

It's a similar deal to how the carbon tax works for households: we pay but we also get a rebate.  So if we pollute more than average, we might pay more than you receive.  If we pollute less than average, we actually receive more than we pay.

For one thing, carbon taxes actually help to make nuclear power more financially attractive, relative to coal power and natural gas (since they create GHG emissions, and nuclear doesn't). At least that's true in the provinces that have lots of coal and natural gas, like a western province known for fossil fuels, a prairie province, and an Atlantic province.

&amp;#x200B;

But the largest provinces in Canada (a central province, a francophone province, and a western coastal province) have extremely clean electricity grids already. The coastal and francophone provinces are around 95% hydro power, and the central province is coal-free and only 10% natural gas, the other 90% is from zero-emission sources (nuclear, hydro, wind, solar).

&amp;#x200B;

Instead, most of our emissions are from transportation, buildings, and industries.

&amp;#x200B;

Governments could mandate the use of specific technologies, to reduce emissions - that's a legitimate option.  But generally they prefer to use less heavy-handed policies, like carbon taxes or regulations, which create incentives (or requirements) to reduce emissions, but allow private companies and people to make their own choices about *how* they want to reduce their emissions.

More like... we're working to kick our own addiction, while continuing to sell to others, knowing that they can easily find another dealer if we stop selling.

&nbsp;

It's the same thing a certain Scandinavian country is doing: they have one of the cleanest electricity grids in the world (98% hydro power), and they lead the world in electric vehicles (over 50% of new cars sold there are electric).  Meanwhile, they produce oil and sell it to the world, which helps them pay for their transition to clean technology.

&gt;the pro pipeline crowd will never vote for him anyway.

Most polls indicate that a majority of people in a certain North American country lean towards being pro-pipeline on the issue of a major energy project.  Presumably that includes lots of people who would consider voting for the incumbent party (who have repeatedly demonstrated their support for the project).  We sometimes get stuck in online arguments between left-wing progressives, committed supporters of the incumbent party and die-hard conservatives, and forget about the huge number of people that are somewhere in the middle, and not committed to a particular party.

&amp;#x200B;

For example, my dad is a man in his 60s, living in a suburb of a major coastal city. He's a swing voter in most elections, and he's pro-pipeline (but also open to environmental measures, including a carbon tax).

&amp;#x200B;

If the incumbent party rejected the pipeline, after previously buying it, I'm confident he would have voted for the opposition in the upcoming election for sure.  Rejecting it would have made them seem too anti-resource development for his liking, and fiscally incompetent (for buying an asset and then devaluing it).

&amp;#x200B;

But by approving it, he'll be more likely to consider voting for the incumbent party, because it legitimizes the view that they're a responsible middle-ground option on the energy-environment debates (not too aggressively anti-development, but still environmentally conscious), and that they think seriously about economic considerations, rather than being beholden to the preferences of lefty activists.

&amp;#x200B;

So no, I don't think today's decision changed the minds of many environmental activists in a major coastal city, or oil workers in a major resource extraction region.  But it might help to shore up the support of moderate voters around the country, particularly in the suburbs of places like that coastal city, a major central city, and a major eastern city.  Places like specific suburban districts in those metropolitan areas.

&gt;It will hurt them badly in a province in Western Canada.

I'm not so sure... keep in mind: the political party already approved it once (December 2016), and then purchased the pipeline to save the project (April/May 2018), before today's announcement.  People who are adamantly opposed to the pipeline should already know that the party supports it.

&amp;#x200B;

By reminding people about the issue, the latest news might push some voters away, but I think most residents of that province had already taken the pipeline issue into account in their impressions of the Prime Minister or their voting intentions.

&amp;#x200B;

It's also worth pointing out that people who are strongly anti-pipeline are a vocal minority in that province, but don't represent the entire population.  There are a lot of people in that province (maybe even a majority) who support the pipeline, or are at least open to it, but they tend not to be as vocal about it (compared to the anti-pipeline residents of that province or pro-pipeline residents of a neighboring province).  Some of those voters may actually be *more* likely to vote for the party, since it shows that they aren't overly anti-resource development.

&amp;#x200B;

We'll see though.  I hopefully we'll get to see some polls in a couple weeks.

